64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,38,"The total number of images in the dataset is 140 335 images after pre-processing and segmentation of the examination lists. 3.3. Statistic analysis In this section, statistics on the database are presented. The database is split into three exclusive parts (training, validation, and test sets). Table 3 shows the uni-, bi-, and tri-grams of these sets and of the full database and also shows how many words, unique words, and a number of characters for all the dataset and the parts. Table 4 shows the out of vocabulary (OOV) statistics of the validation and test sets compared with the training datasets.Table 3 N-gram statistics of the database. Set Word countUnique wordCharacter countUnigramsBigramsTrigrams Training 104 278 24 943 98 258 24 943 100 142 104 242 Validation 22 386 8870 21 054 8870 22 076 22 376 Testing 22 351 8984 21 054 21 054 22 040 22 346 All data 149 015 31 483 140 366 31 483 130 912 147 238 Table 4 N-gram statistics of the database."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,39,"Out of vocabulary OOV tokens OOV percentage Validation 3408 10.36% Testing 3442 10.46% 4. Dataset segmentation Segmentation of text into lines is one of the most important steps in the optical character recognition (OCR) process, in particular, in the optical recognition of document images. Line segmentation is the decomposition of an image containing a sequence of characters into fragments containing individual characters. There are several works on line-level or full page-level [66,67]. but for us, it is better to work on word level because we annotate the dataset by words not by line or paragraph. So, in our case, for line segmentation, we used a genetic algorithm. Genetic Algorithm (GA) is a classic evolutionary algorithm based on random enumeration of a parameter. By random here we mean that in order to find a solution using GA, random changes were applied to the current solutions to generate new ones. It is a slow, gradual process that works by making small and slow changes."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,40,"In addition, GA is slowly making small changes to its decisions until it gets the best solution. The chromosome in our case is the red line that we draw in Fig. 5. The chromosome is made up of genes. Each gene in our algorithm is represented as a line with a specific length, 50px by default, and a random height between two lines. A population is a set of generations of chromosomes, that is, a set of dividing lines between two lines. The fitness function of the algorithm was represented by the sum of all horizontal and connecting lines. Before using the picture, we made an inversion and added a unit to the image matrix to make it convenient to count. The white color in the picture has a weight of 1, and the black color has a weight of 256. The lower the value of the fitness function, the higher the quality of the solution. When parents are crossed, offspring take 50% of their genes from one parent and another 50% from the other parent."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,41,"For every two parents, crossing occurs by sequential exchange of genes in the chromosome, starting from the beginning or from the end. The resulting chromosomes are descendants. Mutation of individuals occurred with a 20% probability. Initial parameters of the genetic algorithm: Number of populations: 80 Gene Length: 50 px Generation: 100 To implement the genetic algorithm, first of all, we need to determine the coordinates from where we will start making changes. For correct segmentation, the coordinates must lie between two lines. To determine the extreme points, we get a histogram of the picture in height. The histogram gives us the sum of the pixels of each line of the image, to get explicit vertices, we smooth the data using a Gaussian filter. The result is shown in Fig. 6. Let us draw lines at the maximum (blue) and minimum (green) points on the histogram, we get Fig. 7. Our coordinates will lie between the blue lines."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,42,"After that, we run the genetic algorithm and get the line segmentation. The result is in Fig. 8. Since the genetic algorithm is an iterative task, it takes a lot of 6N. Toiganbayeva, M. Kasem, G. Abdimanap et al. Signal Processing: Image Communication 108 (2022) 116827 Fig. 4. Histogram of Characters in the dataset. Fig. 5. Image histogram by height. Fig. 6. Smoothed image histogram by height. time, most of the time is the calculation of fitness functions. To reduce processing time, we used the numba library for fitness functions and reduced the processing speed by a factor of 27 (190 s VS 7 s for a single image). We also used the pymp library for parallelization, the total time was reduced to 3.5 s. for one image. After receiving the lines, we need to segment the words, since our model accepts words as input. We will do the segmentation of words in the same way, through histograms. First, draw a histogram horizontally and smooth it with a Gaussian filter."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,43,"Find the vertices and draw in the picture. We get the result as in Fig. 95. Experimental results A quantitative comparison of well-known recurrent neural networks (RNN), such as Bluche [21], Puigcerver [22], Flor [23] and Abdallah [24] models, has been implemented to choose the best performing model on the dataset given. At the first, the final dataset was split into three datasets as follows: Training (70%), Validation (15%), and Testing (15%). After training, validation, and testing datasets were prepared, the models were trained, and a series of comparative evaluation experiments were conducted. As experiment results proved, the Flor model demonstrated the best performance with a 6.52% character error rate (CER), 24.52% word error rate (WER), and 26.98% SER for the test dataset. 5.1."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,44,"Evaluation methods We used two ways to evaluate models in this article: For the results reported in the first technique, established performance measurements such as the character error rate (CER) and word error rate (WER) [68] are employed. The Levenshtein distance is calculated by dividing the total number of characters in the ground truth word ( ) by the amount of character substitution ( ), insertion ( ), and deletions ( ) required to convert one string into another. =++ (1) Similarly, the   is determined by the sum of the number of term substitutions ( ), insertions ( ), and deletions ( ) required for the transformation of one string into another, and divided by the total number of ground-truth terms ( ). =++ (2) 7N. Toiganbayeva, M. Kasem, G. Abdimanap et al. Signal Processing: Image Communication 108 (2022) 116827 Fig. 7. Line drawing. 5.2. Training Tensorflow [69], a Python-based deep learning library, was used to train all of the models."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,45,"Through Python, Tensorflow allows for the transparent usage of highly efficient mathematical operations on GPUs. In the Python script, a computational graph is built to define all operations required for the given computations. The machine that was carried out the tests with 2x Intel(R) Xeon(R) E-5-2680 processors, 4x NVIDIA Tesla k20x graphics cards, and 100 GB of RAM. The usage of a GPU reduced model training time by about a factor of three, but this speed-up was not extensively evaluated during the project, so it may have varied. The reports plots were built with the Python package matplotlib, and the visuals were developed with Inkscape, a vector graphics program akin to Adobe Photoshop. The validation loss value is minimized in all models. The RMSProp method [70] is used to execute the stochastic gradient descent optimization using a base learning rate of 0.001 and 32 mini-batches."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,46,"We also used early stopping with patience 20 since we wanted to track the validation loss at each epoch and terminate training if the validation loss did not improve after 20 epochs. 5.3. Proposed models In this section, we will present deep learning models based on RNN and CTC loss function which has been implemented to choose the bestperforming model on the dataset given. Bluche [21] built a deep neural network that could split into three main parts, First convolutional layers as the encoder of the input images. It operates two-dimensional representations and supplies 2D features maps. This part contains about 20% of the models free parameters but represents the slowest component of their architecture. Aims to make it generic to be reusable to factorize the processing time. The second is the interface that transforms the 2D image-like representation into 1D representation."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,47,"The third is the decoder, a bidirectional Long Short Term Memory Recurrent Neural Network (LSTM RNN) that processes feature sequences to predict a sequence of characters. This part has the most capacity of the network about 80% but has fast processing. Puigcerver [22] present a neural network architecture based on convolutional and 1D-LSTM layers to make line-level Handwritten Text Recognition (HTR). Then providing statistically sound empirical study that proves the architecture gives similar or better accuracy compared with the state of the art 2D-LSTM architecture and incomparably faster. And showing that performing appropriate random distortions on the training images, reduce the error rates."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,48,"Flor [23] proposed New Gated CRNN architecture used for offline Handwritten Text Recognition (HTR) systems, by using the latest machine learning techniques and approaches in the field of Natural Language Processing (NLP), like the Gated mechanism presented by Dauphin [71], and Bidirectional Gated Recurrent Units (BGRU) [72], Flor proposed Gated-CNN-BGRU model which involves a few parameters and achieves a low error rate in the text recognition process. 8N. Toiganbayeva, M. Kasem, G. Abdimanap et al. Signal Processing: Image Communication 108 (2022) 116827 Fig. 8. Genetic Algorithm Result, String Segmentation. Fig. 9. Segmentation of words. There are more contributions with the following aspects, handling long sentences with different styles, noise, and variations, even in the case of limited training data. Improving the recognition results through the new Gated-CNN-BGRU architecture."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,49,"Reducing the number of trainable parameters, making the model smaller and with lower computational cost. Abdallah [24], aiming at improving HTR model accuracy in the handwritten Cyrillic text recognition task. This models architecture consists of 4 main parts: encoder, attention, decoder, and CTC. An encoder part consists of 5 convolutional blocks, each of which is made up of a convolutional layer, Parametric Rectified Linear Unit(PReLU) activator [73] with Batch Normalization, and gated convolutional layer [21]. The Dropout technique is also applied at the input of some convolutional layers (with a dropout probability of 0.5) to reduce the overfitting issue [74]. As an attention part of this models architecture, the Bahdanau attention mechanism is used [75]. Generally, attention mechanisms encode an input sentence by segmenting it into a fixed number of parts so they can be processed later by a decoder."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,50,"Bahdanaus attention mechanism enabled the attention mechanism to focus on relevant parts of an input sentence, rather than hard segmenting it. The key role of the Bahdanau attention mechanism applied between an encoder and decoder is to provide a richer encoding of the input sequence. 5.4. Results All the models were trained on the KOHTD dataset. We evaluated these models by the standard performance measures used for all results presented: CER, WER, and SER. For all models, the minibatch of 32 size 9N. Toiganbayeva, M. Kasem, G. Abdimanap et al. Signal Processing: Image Communication 108 (2022) 116827 Fig. 10. Models losses. and Early Stopping after 20 epochs without improvement in validation loss value and lr =0.001 were set. For the best use of each model, within the 20 tolerance epochs, ReduceLRonPlateau schedule [76] with a decay factor of 0.2 after 10 epochs without improvement in validation loss value was also used."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,51,"All of the following figures present the character error rate, which shows how the model detects each character. The first experiment was conducted with the Bluche model which showed a performance with an average of 8.36% WER, 28.95% CER, and 31.45% SER on the test dataset. Fig. 10(a) shows the training and validation losses of the Bluche model. The next experiment was carried out with the Flor model which was trained on data for 186 epochs. This model demonstrated performance with an average of 6.52% CER, 24.52% WER, and 26.98% SER on the test dataset. Fig. 10(b) shows the training and validation losses of the Flor model. The same experiments were conducted with the Abdallah HTR model. Unlike previous models examined, this model showed a lower average of 8.22% CER, 22.60% WER, and 25.22% SER on the test dataset. Fig. 10(c) shows the training and validation losses of the Abdallah model. Also, we conducted two other experiments with Carion [77], Scheidl [78] models."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,52,"these models demonstrated performance with an average of 12.85% CER, 31.73% WER, and 35.16% SER on the test dataset for the Carion model and 22.99% CER, 62.77% WER, and 62.77% SER for Scheidl model.Table 5 CER, WER, and SER for Bluche, Puigcerver, Flor, and Abdallah. Model Method CER WER SER Flor [23] Gated-CNN 6.52% 24.52% 26.98% Puigcerver [22]2-LSTM 8.01% 26.34% 28.94% Abdallah [24] Attention-Gated-CNN-BGRU 8.22% 22.60% 25.22% Bluche [21] Gated-CRNN 8.36% 28.95% 31.45% Carion [77] Transformer 12.85% 31.73% 35.16% Scheidl [78] CNN-LSTM-CTC 22.99% 62.77%  The last experiment was carried out with the Puigcerver model which was trained on data for 135 epochs. This model demonstrated performance with an average of 8.01% CER, 26.34% WER, and 28.94% SER on the test dataset. Fig. 10(d) shows the training and validation losses of the Puigcerver model. Table 5 shows the result of the comparison between all models. 6."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,53,"Conclusion and future work In this research work, firstly, we have built the Kazakh Offline Handwritten Text Dataset (KOHTD). The dataset can serve as a basis for research in handwriting recognition. This consists of a large collection of exam papers filled by students at Satbayev University and Al-Farabi 10N. Toiganbayeva, M. Kasem, G. Abdimanap et al. Signal Processing: Image Communication 108 (2022) 116827 Kazakh National University.also We present a comparison between the most popular Handwritten datasets. Secondly, for line segmentation, we used a genetic algorithm. Genetic Algorithm (GA) is a classic evolutionary algorithm based on random enumeration of a parameter. By random here we mean that in order to find a solution using GA, random changes were applied to the current solutions to generate new ones. It is a slow, gradual process that works by making small and slow changes. In addition, GA is slowly making small changes to its decisions until it gets the best solution."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,54,"Finally, We used two ways to evaluate models in this article: For the results reported in the first technique, established performance measurements such as the character error rate (CER) and word error rate (WER) are employed. also, we present deep learning models based on RNN and CTC loss function which has been implemented to choose the best performing model on the dataset. this research work tried to solve a handwritten Kazakh interpretation task using well-known RNN models, such as Flor, Abdallah, Bluche, and Puigcerver HTR models. These RNN models were first quantitatively evaluated against each other to select the best-performing one. According to experiments, the Flor HTR model demonstrated the highest recognition rate overall. As experiment results proved, the Flor model demonstrated the best performance with a 6.52% character error rate (CER), 24.52% word error rate (WER), and 26.98% SER for the test dataset."
64_2022_KOHTD Kazakh offline handwritten text dataset.pdf,55,"One of the purposes of this research was to look into and compare state-of-the-art CRNN models in order to see which one performed best in a handwritten recognition challenge. This goal also considers all efforts to improve the best-performing CRNN model. In future work, The physical look of handwriting reveals the relationship between gender and handwriting. So we will present information about the gender to use for the classification of gender based on handwriting and writer identification. Also provide full page of exam so researcher can used it for annotation and full page entity recognition. CRediT authorship contribution statement Nazgul Toiganbayeva: Conceptualization, Methodology, Validation, Formal analysis, Investigation, Resources, Data curation, Writingoriginal draft preparation supervision, Writingoriginal draft preparation."
